{"name":"Irish Folklore","hex":"#d3e3bf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"folklore","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The tales, legends, superstitions, and traditions of a particular ethnic population.","The tales, superstitions etc. of any particular group or community."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/folklore"}],"entries":[{"title":"Irish folklore","blocks":[{"text":"Irish folklore refers to the folktales, balladry, music, dance and mythology of Ireland. It is the study and appreciation of how people lived. The stories are usually told to explain the unexplainable."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_folklore","wikidata":"Q55647961"},{"title":"Irish mythology","blocks":[{"text":"Irish mythology is the body of myths indigenous to the island of Ireland. It was originally passed down orally in the prehistoric era. In the early medieval era, myths were written down by Christian scribes, who revised it considerably in order to fit the history and religion according to Christianized learning. Irish mythology is the best-preserved branch of Celtic mythology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Riders_of_th_Sidhe_%28big%29.jpg/330px-Riders_of_th_Sidhe_%28big%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_mythology","wikidata":"Q1471505"},{"title":"Folklore","blocks":[{"text":"Folklore is the body of expressive culture shared by a particular group of people, culture or subculture. This includes oral traditions such as tales, legends, proverbs, poems, and jokes. This also includes material culture, such as traditional building styles common to the group. Folklore also encompasses customary lore, taking actions for folk beliefs, including folk religion, and the forms and rituals of celebrations such as festivals, weddings, folk dances, and initiation rites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Hansel-and-gretel-rackham.jpg/330px-Hansel-and-gretel-rackham.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folklore","wikidata":"Q36192"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Hope-coventina01a.jpg/500px-Hope-coventina01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Hope-coventina01a.jpg/500px-Hope-coventina01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHope-coventina01a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Banshee.jpg/500px-Banshee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Banshee.jpg/1280px-Banshee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Banshee.jpg/500px-Banshee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bunworth Banshee, Fairy Legends and Traditions of the South of Ireland by Thomas Crofton Croker, 1825","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABanshee.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Fairy_Trees_near_Greenan_-_geograph.org.uk_-_962666.jpg/500px-Fairy_Trees_near_Greenan_-_geograph.org.uk_-_962666.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Fairy_Trees_near_Greenan_-_geograph.org.uk_-_962666.jpg/500px-Fairy_Trees_near_Greenan_-_geograph.org.uk_-_962666.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fairy Trees near Greenan. According to fairy lore, the hawthorn tree, also known as a fairy tree, is said to mark the terri